  "body": {
    "id": "lem:coverage-testing-inequality",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "Let \\(C_n\\) be any interval satisfying the coverage constraint in \\(def:minimax-honest-length\\). For every pair \\(\\Pi_0,\\Pi_1\\) and target sets \\(\\mathcal T_0,\\mathcal T_1\\) satisfying \\(def:prior-predictive-pair\\), \\(E_{\\mathbb Q_0}[|C_n|]\\ge\\Delta(\\mathcal T_0,\\mathcal T_1)\\{1-2\\alpha-\\|\\mathbb Q_0-\\mathbb Q_1\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\}_+\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(n\\)",
      "\\(\\alpha\\)",
      "\\(\\Pi_0,\\Pi_1\\)",
      "\\(\\mathbb Q_0,\\mathbb Q_1\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal T_0,\\mathcal T_1\\)",
      "\\(\\Delta(\\mathcal T_0,\\mathcal T_1)\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:minimax-honest-length",
      "def:prior-predictive-pair"
    ],
    "justification": "Uniform coverage forces a long interval whenever the observed experiments are close but their ATE sets are separated.",
    "gap": "Low1997 and CaiLow2004, Theorem 1, establish coverage-to-length inequalities in nonparametric Gaussian models; this lemma states the experiment-level version needed for prior mixtures in the discrete causal model.",
    "consumer": "The direct confidence-length converse, without reducing interval performance to squared-error risk.",
    "proof_tex": "Let\n\\[\n A_j:=\\{C_n\\cap\\mathcal T_j\\ne\\varnothing\\},\\qquad j\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\nUniform coverage and the support condition in\n\\(def:prior\\text{-}predictive\\text{-}pair\\) imply\n\\[\n \\mathbb Q_j(A_j)\n \\ge\\int Q_P^{(n)}\\{\\tau(P)\\in C_n\\}\\,d\\Pi_j(P)\n \\ge1-\\alpha.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nBy the defining variational inequality for total variation,\n\\[\n \\mathbb Q_0(A_1)\\ge \\mathbb Q_1(A_1)\n -\\|\\mathbb Q_0-\\mathbb Q_1\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n \\ge1-\\alpha-\\|\\mathbb Q_0-\\mathbb Q_1\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe union bound applied under \\(\\mathbb Q_0\\) to (1)--(2) gives\n\\[\n \\mathbb Q_0(A_0\\cap A_1)\n \\ge1-2\\alpha-\\|\\mathbb Q_0-\\mathbb Q_1\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nOn \\(A_0\\cap A_1\\), the interval \\(C_n\\) contains a point of each target\nset.  Because it is an interval, its length is at least\n\\(\\Delta(\\mathcal T_0,\\mathcal T_1)\\).  Multiplying this deterministic\ninequality by \\(\\mathbf1_{A_0\\cap A_1}\\), taking \\(\\mathbb Q_0\\)-expectations,\nand replacing the right side of (3) by its positive part proves the claim.",
